<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Institutio Oratoria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 95</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Institutio Oratoria is a twelve-book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> treatise on the education and training of the orator, written by Quintilian, a rhetorician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calagurris">Calagurris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, around 95 CE. It covers a speaker's schooling from childhood and the theory and practice of rhetoric. The rediscovery of a complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in 1416 made it a standard text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> education.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/institutio-oratoria.jpg" alt="Institutio Oratoria" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scanned by Aristeas (Roman Eisele). Copper engraving by F. Bleyswyk. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quintilian,_Institutio_oratoria_ed._Burman_(Leiden_1720),_frontispiece,_black_and_white.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
